window.COURSE={"books":{"S1":{"name":"Asan Sarf I","ar":"آسان صرف — حصہ اوّل","file":"ASAN-SARF-1.pdf","desc":"Build words from roots","count":18,"pages":40},"S2":{"name":"Asan Sarf II","ar":"آسان صرف — حصہ دوم","file":"ASAN-SARF-2.pdf","desc":"Master patterns and derivation","count":13,"pages":65},"N1":{"name":"Asan Nahw I","ar":"آسان نحو — حصہ اوّل","file":"ASAN_NAHW_1.pdf","desc":"Connect words into sentences","count":15,"pages":41},"N2":{"name":"Asan Nahw II","ar":"آسان نحو — حصہ دوم","file":"ASAN_NAHW_2.pdf","desc":"Understand grammatical governance","count":23,"pages":105}},"lessons":[{"id":1,"book":"S1","pages":"3","section":"1","ar":"حَرَكَاتٌ","title":"Vowels & signs","rule":"Sarf studies how a word changes form. A fatḥah, kasrah or ḍammah changes the sound; sukūn marks the absence of a vowel and shaddah doubles a consonant.","urdu":"زبر، زیر، پیش، سکون اور تشدید کی پہچان۔","examples":[{"ar":"بَ","label":"Fatḥah","note":"The fatḥah sits above the consonant and gives a short a sound."},{"ar":"بِ","label":"Kasrah","note":"The kasrah sits below the consonant and gives a short i sound."},{"ar":"بُ","label":"Ḍammah","note":"The ḍammah sits above the consonant and gives a short u sound."},{"ar":"بْ","label":"Sukūn","note":"Sukūn marks the absence of a following short vowel: read b, not ba, bi or bu."},{"ar":"بَّ","label":"Shaddah","note":"Shaddah doubles the consonant; the fatḥah above it gives the second occurrence its vowel."},{"ar":"كِتَابٌ / كِتَابًا / كِتَابٍ","label":"Tanwīn","note":"Two ḍammahs, fatḥahs or kasrahs represent a final n sound in connected speech."}],"question":"Which has sukūn?","correct":"بْ","options":["بْ","بَ","بُ"],"feedback":"Sukūn is the small circle above the letter.","mode":"vowels"},{"id":2,"book":"S1","pages":"4","section":"2","ar":"أَقْسَامُ الْفِعْلِ","title":"Verb types","rule":"Māḍī points to the past, muḍāriʿ to present or future, amr requests an action and nahy forbids it. Maʿrūf names the doer in the grammatical construction; majhūl does not.","urdu":"ماضی، مضارع، امر، نہی، معروف اور مجہول۔","examples":[{"ar":"ضَرَبَ","label":"Past","note":"The action has occurred: he struck."},{"ar":"يَضْرِبُ","label":"Present/future","note":"The prefix يـ and the present pattern place the action in the present or future."},{"ar":"اِضْرِبْ","label":"Command","note":"The command addresses the listener directly: strike!"},{"ar":"لَا تَضْرِبْ","label":"Prohibition","note":"The prohibitive لَا plus a jussive verb asks the listener not to strike."},{"ar":"ضُرِبَ","label":"Passive past","note":"The doer is not expressed as the grammatical subject: he was struck."},{"ar":"مَا ضَرَبَ","label":"Negative past","note":"مَا negates the past: he did not strike."}],"question":"Which is a command?","correct":"اِضْرِبْ","options":["اِضْرِبْ","ضَرَبَ","يَضْرِبُ"],"feedback":"Amr requests the action.","mode":"timeline"},{"id":3,"book":"S1","pages":"4-6","section":"3–4","ar":"الصِّيَغُ","title":"The fourteen conjugation slots","rule":"Each slot combines person, gender and number. Third person: 6; second person: 6; first person: 2. The masculine and feminine dual second-person forms are identical, but are counted separately. First person is shared by both genders; نَحْنُ includes two or more speakers.","urdu":"غائب، حاضر، متکلم؛ واحد، تثنیہ، جمع؛ مذکر، مؤنث۔","examples":[{"ar":"هُوَ","label":"Third person masculine singular","note":"He. Slot 1 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلَ; in the present active pattern it is يَفْعُلُ."},{"ar":"هُمَا","label":"Third person masculine dual","note":"They two (masculine). Slot 2 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلَا; in the present active pattern it is يَفْعُلَانِ."},{"ar":"هُمْ","label":"Third person masculine plural","note":"They (masculine). Slot 3 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلُوا; in the present active pattern it is يَفْعُلُونَ."},{"ar":"هِيَ","label":"Third person feminine singular","note":"She. Slot 4 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلَتْ; in the present active pattern it is تَفْعُلُ."},{"ar":"هُمَا","label":"Third person feminine dual","note":"They two (feminine). Slot 5 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلَتَا; in the present active pattern it is تَفْعُلَانِ."},{"ar":"هُنَّ","label":"Third person feminine plural","note":"They (feminine). Slot 6 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلْنَ; in the present active pattern it is يَفْعُلْنَ."},{"ar":"أَنْتَ","label":"Second person masculine singular","note":"You (one male). Slot 7 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلْتَ; in the present active pattern it is تَفْعُلُ."},{"ar":"أَنْتُمَا","label":"Second person masculine dual","note":"You two (male). Slot 8 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلْتُمَا; in the present active pattern it is تَفْعُلَانِ."},{"ar":"أَنْتُمْ","label":"Second person masculine plural","note":"You (men). Slot 9 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلْتُمْ; in the present active pattern it is تَفْعُلُونَ."},{"ar":"أَنْتِ","label":"Second person feminine singular","note":"You (one female). Slot 10 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلْتِ; in the present active pattern it is تَفْعُلِينَ."},{"ar":"أَنْتُمَا","label":"Second person feminine dual","note":"You two (female). Slot 11 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلْتُمَا; in the present active pattern it is تَفْعُلَانِ."},{"ar":"أَنْتُنَّ","label":"Second person feminine plural","note":"You (women). Slot 12 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلْتُنَّ; in the present active pattern it is تَفْعُلْنَ."},{"ar":"أَنَا","label":"First person singular","note":"I. Slot 13 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلْتُ; in the present active pattern it is أَفْعُلُ."},{"ar":"نَحْنُ","label":"First person plural (including dual)","note":"We (two or more). Slot 14 of 14. In the past active pattern the corresponding form is فَعَلْنَا; in the present active pattern it is نَفْعُلُ."}],"question":"How many traditional slots are there?","correct":"14","options":["14","12","6"],"feedback":"Six third-person, six second-person and two first-person slots.","mode":"people"},{"id":4,"book":"S1","pages":"7-8","section":"5–6","ar":"الْمَاضِي الْمَعْرُوفُ","title":"Past active","rule":"Keep the root and adjust endings for the person. The middle root letter can have fatḥah, kasrah or ḍammah according to the verb.","urdu":"ماضی معروف کی گردان میں صیغے کے مطابق آخر بدلتا ہے۔","examples":[{"ar":"فَعَلَ","label":"He did","note":"This is the basic third-person masculine singular form."},{"ar":"فَعَلَا","label":"They two did","note":"Add the dual alif to show that two males performed the action."},{"ar":"فَعَلُوا","label":"They men did","note":"Add وا for the third-person masculine plural; the final written alif is silent here."},{"ar":"فَعَلْتُ","label":"I did","note":"The root stays recognizable, but the suffix تُ identifies the first-person singular."},{"ar":"فَعِلَ / فَعُلَ","label":"Other past stem vowels","note":"The second radical can take kasrah or ḍammah, in addition to fatḥah. The conjugation endings follow the same basic past paradigm."}],"question":"Which means I did?","correct":"فَعَلْتُ","options":["فَعَلْتُ","فَعَلْتَ","فَعَلُوا"],"feedback":"The ending تُ marks first-person singular.","mode":"flow"},{"id":5,"book":"S1","pages":"9","section":"7","ar":"الْمَاضِي الْمَجْهُولُ","title":"Past passive","rule":"For a regular triliteral verb, put ḍammah on the first radical and kasrah on the second. The final letter follows the conjugation.","urdu":"ماضی مجہول: پہلے حرف پر پیش اور دوسرے پر زیر۔","examples":[{"ar":"ضَرَبَ","label":"Active","note":"Notice the two fatḥahs of the regular active example."},{"ar":"ضُرِبَ","label":"Passive","note":"Change the first radical to ḍammah and second to kasrah: the doer is not named by this passive form."},{"ar":"كَتَبَ","label":"Active","note":"The same active template works with a different set of radicals, ك ت ب."},{"ar":"كُتِبَ","label":"Passive","note":"Apply فُعِلَ to ك ت ب: كُتِبَ, it was written."}],"question":"Passive of كَتَبَ?","correct":"كُتِبَ","options":["كُتِبَ","كَتَبَ","يُكْتَبُ"],"feedback":"The past passive pattern is فُعِلَ.","mode":"flow"},{"id":6,"book":"S1","pages":"10-11","section":"8","ar":"الْمَاضِي الْمَنْفِيُّ","title":"Negative past","rule":"Place مَا before the past verb to deny the action; keep the active or passive pattern intact.","urdu":"ماضی مثبت کے شروع میں مَا لائیں۔","examples":[{"ar":"نَصَرَ","label":"He helped","note":"The positive past says that he helped."},{"ar":"مَا نَصَرَ","label":"He did not help","note":"Attach the independent negative particle مَا before the verb. The verb itself stays active."},{"ar":"نُصِرَ","label":"He was helped","note":"The passive past reports that someone was helped."},{"ar":"مَا نُصِرَ","label":"He was not helped","note":"Add مَا to the passive form; the passive vowels remain unchanged."},{"ar":"مَا فُعِلَ","label":"Negative passive","note":"The negative particle leaves the passive vowels unchanged."}],"question":"Negative active of نَصَرَ?","correct":"مَا نَصَرَ","options":["مَا نَصَرَ","مَا نُصِرَ","لَنْ يَنْصُرَ"],"feedback":"مَا negates the past form without changing voice.","mode":"timeline"},{"id":7,"book":"S1","pages":"11-13","section":"9–10","ar":"الْمُضَارِعُ الْمَعْرُوفُ","title":"Present active","rule":"Muḍāriʿ uses the prefixes أ، ن، ي، ت and appropriate endings. Its middle vowel depends on the lexical bāb.","urdu":"علامات مضارع: ا، ن، ی، ت۔","examples":[{"ar":"يَنْصُرُ","label":"He helps","note":"The prefix يـ identifies the third-person masculine singular here."},{"ar":"تَنْصُرُ","label":"She/you help","note":"The prefix تـ can identify a female third person or a male second person in this form; context disambiguates."},{"ar":"أَنْصُرُ","label":"I help","note":"The initial hamzah identifies the singular speaker."},{"ar":"نَنْصُرُ","label":"We help","note":"The نـ prefix identifies the first-person plural."},{"ar":"يَفْعُلَانِ","label":"Dual ending","note":"The indicative dual ending is ـَانِ, as in the two masculine third-person forms."},{"ar":"تَفْعُلِينَ","label":"Feminine singular addressee","note":"The indicative feminine singular second person ends in ـِينَ."},{"ar":"يَفْعُلْنَ","label":"Feminine plural","note":"The feminine plural ends in nūn al-niswah; it is not the same nūn as the five indicative forms."}],"question":"Which means we help?","correct":"نَنْصُرُ","options":["نَنْصُرُ","أَنْصُرُ","يَنْصُرُ"],"feedback":"نَ is the first-person plural prefix.","mode":"flow"},{"id":8,"book":"S1","pages":"13-15","section":"11","ar":"الْمُضَارِعُ الْمَجْهُولُ","title":"Present passive & negation","rule":"For a regular form I passive, the prefix has ḍammah and the penultimate radical has fatḥah. لَا can negate the present.","urdu":"مضارع مجہول میں علامت مضارع پر پیش اور ماقبل آخر پر زبر۔","examples":[{"ar":"يَكْتُبُ","label":"He writes","note":"The present active form identifies an action performed by the subject."},{"ar":"يُكْتَبُ","label":"It is written","note":"The prefix vowel becomes ḍammah and the penultimate radical takes fatḥah: يُكْتَبُ."},{"ar":"لَا يَكْتُبُ","label":"He does not write","note":"Ordinary negative لَا denies the ongoing action; it does not make this indicative form jussive."},{"ar":"لَا يُكْتَبُ","label":"Negative passive present","note":"Ordinary negative lā does not make the present jussive: it is not written."}],"question":"Present passive of يَكْتُبُ?","correct":"يُكْتَبُ","options":["يُكْتَبُ","يَكْتُبُ","كُتِبَ"],"feedback":"The pattern is يُفْعَلُ.","mode":"flow"},{"id":9,"book":"S1","pages":"15-16","section":"12–13","ar":"النَّفْيُ بِلَنْ وَلَمْ","title":"Future and past negation","rule":"لَنْ negates the future and governs naṣb; لَمْ negates a past event using the jussive muḍāriʿ.","urdu":"لَنْ سے مستقبل کی نفی، لَمْ سے ماضی کی نفی۔","examples":[{"ar":"يَنْصُرُ","label":"He helps","note":"Start with the indicative present, ending in ḍammah."},{"ar":"لَنْ يَنْصُرَ","label":"He will not help","note":"لَنْ puts the regular present verb into naṣb, here marked by fatḥah, and denies future action."},{"ar":"لَمْ يَنْصُرْ","label":"He did not help","note":"لَمْ governs jazm, here marked by sukūn, while its meaning refers to the past."},{"ar":"لَنْ يَفْعُلُوا","label":"Subjunctive plural","note":"With لَنْ the indicative suffix ـُونَ loses its final nūn."},{"ar":"لَمْ يَفْعُلُوا","label":"Jussive plural","note":"With لَمْ the same indicative nūn is dropped, but the meaning negates the past."}],"question":"Which uses jazm?","correct":"لَمْ يَنْصُرْ","options":["لَمْ يَنْصُرْ","لَنْ يَنْصُرَ","يَنْصُرُ"],"feedback":"لَمْ makes the regular singular ending sākin.","mode":"timeline"},{"id":10,"book":"S1","pages":"17-18","section":"14","ar":"نُونُ التَّأْكِيدِ","title":"Emphatic nūn","rule":"The heavy nūn نَّ and light nūn نْ give emphasis to suitable verb forms. Study the full table for suffix changes.","urdu":"نون تاکید ثقیلہ اور خفیفہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"لَيَفْعَلَنَّ","label":"Heavy emphasis","note":"The doubled nūn with shaddah is the heavy emphatic suffix; the preceding vowel changes as required."},{"ar":"لَيَفْعَلَنْ","label":"Light emphasis","note":"The light emphatic nūn has sukūn, not shaddah. Both forms reinforce the assertion."}],"question":"Which is heavy emphasis?","correct":"لَيَفْعَلَنَّ","options":["لَيَفْعَلَنَّ","لَيَفْعَلَنْ","يَفْعَلُ"],"feedback":"Heavy nūn carries shaddah.","mode":"vowels"},{"id":11,"book":"S1","pages":"19-20","section":"15–16","ar":"الْأَمْرُ","title":"Commands","rule":"For regular direct commands, start with the second-person muḍāriʿ in jazm, remove the prefix, and add hamzat al-waṣl when the remaining word begins with sukūn. Other command forms use lām.","urdu":"حاضر کے مضارع سے امر بنائیں؛ ضرورت پر ہمزۂ وصل لگائیں۔","examples":[{"ar":"تَكْتُبُ","label":"You write","note":"Begin with the form addressed to one male listener, تَكْتُبُ."},{"ar":"تَكْتُبْ","label":"Jussive","note":"Change the indicative ending to sukūn, yielding the jussive تَكْتُبْ."},{"ar":"كْتُبْ","label":"Remove prefix","note":"Remove the initial مضارع prefix تَ. The remainder begins with a consonant carrying sukūn."},{"ar":"اُكْتُبْ","label":"Add hamzah","note":"Add hamzat al-waṣl with ḍammah because the middle radical of this present stem has ḍammah: اُكْتُبْ."},{"ar":"اِضْرِبْ","label":"Imperative with kasrah","note":"The hamzat al-waṣl is given kasrah for this regular stem, because its imperfect middle radical does not have ḍammah."},{"ar":"لِيَفْعُلْ","label":"Indirect command","note":"Lām of command plus a jussive imperfect expresses a command concerning someone else."}],"question":"Command of تَكْتُبُ?","correct":"اُكْتُبْ","options":["اُكْتُبْ","تَكْتُبْ","اِكْتُبْ"],"feedback":"The middle vowel is ḍammah, so initial hamzah takes ḍammah.","mode":"flow"},{"id":12,"book":"S1","pages":"20-22","section":"17","ar":"أَمْرٌ مُؤَكَّدٌ","title":"Emphatic commands","rule":"Add the appropriate emphatic nūn to a command; the suffix and vowels change according to the form.","urdu":"امر میں نون تاکید کے ساتھ گردان کی مشق کریں۔","examples":[{"ar":"اُكْتُبْ","label":"Write","note":"First establish the ordinary singular command."},{"ar":"اُكْتُبَنَّ","label":"Definitely write","note":"The heavy nūn joins the command and changes the preceding vowel to fatḥah in this form."},{"ar":"اُكْتُبَنْ","label":"Light emphasis","note":"The light nūn has sukūn; compare the two suffixes aloud."}],"question":"Which has heavy emphasis?","correct":"اُكْتُبَنَّ","options":["اُكْتُبَنَّ","اُكْتُبْ","اُكْتُبَنْ"],"feedback":"The doubled nūn marks heavy emphasis.","mode":"vowels"},{"id":13,"book":"S1","pages":"22-24","section":"18","ar":"النَّهْيُ","title":"Prohibition","rule":"Use prohibitive لَا with the jussive present; distinguish it from ordinary negative لَا.","urdu":"لَا ناہیہ کے بعد مضارع مجزوم ہوتا ہے۔","examples":[{"ar":"تَكْتُبُ","label":"You write","note":"Identify the second-person present form first."},{"ar":"لَا تَكْتُبْ","label":"Do not write","note":"Prohibitive لَا requires jazm, so the singular form ends in sukūn."},{"ar":"لَا تَكْتُبُوا","label":"Do not write (plural)","note":"In the masculine plural the indicative nūn disappears; the wāw remains with the written alif."},{"ar":"لَا تَفْعُلِي","label":"Feminine singular prohibition","note":"The indicative تَفْعُلِينَ loses its final nūn in the jussive."},{"ar":"لَا تَفْعُلْنَ","label":"Feminine plural prohibition","note":"Nūn al-niswah remains; the form is built on sukūn."}],"question":"Which is a prohibition?","correct":"لَا تَكْتُبْ","options":["لَا تَكْتُبْ","لَا تَكْتُبُ","اُكْتُبْ"],"feedback":"Prohibitive lā governs jazm.","mode":"flow"},{"id":14,"book":"S1","pages":"24-29","section":"19–23","ar":"أَنْوَاعُ الْمَاضِي","title":"Five further past constructions","rule":"Compare the book’s near, remote, continuous, probable and wished-for past forms using particles or كَانَ. Read the tables for positive and negative voices.","urdu":"ماضی قریب، بعید، استمراری، احتمالی اور تمنائی۔","examples":[{"ar":"قَدْ كَتَبَ","label":"Near past","note":"قَدْ with the past can express a completed or recent action in context."},{"ar":"كَانَ قَدْ كَتَبَ","label":"Remote past","note":"كَانَ قَدْ places the completion before a past reference point."},{"ar":"كَانَ يَكْتُبُ","label":"Continuous past","note":"كَانَ followed by the present verb describes a continuing or habitual past action."},{"ar":"لَعَلَّهُ كَتَبَ","label":"Probable past","note":"لَعَلَّ with its pronoun expresses hope or likelihood about the event."},{"ar":"لَيْتَهُ كَتَبَ","label":"Wished past","note":"لَيْتَ expresses a wish concerning the event."},{"ar":"كَتَبَ","label":"Absolute past","note":"Simple past: he wrote, without specifying how recently."}],"question":"Which expresses ongoing past action?","correct":"كَانَ يَكْتُبُ","options":["كَانَ يَكْتُبُ","قَدْ كَتَبَ","لَيْتَهُ كَتَبَ"],"feedback":"Kāna with muḍāriʿ expresses past continuity.","mode":"timeline"},{"id":15,"book":"S1","pages":"30","section":"24","ar":"اسْمُ الْفَاعِلِ وَالْمَفْعُولِ","title":"Active and passive participles","rule":"Regular triliteral active participles follow فَاعِلٌ and passive participles follow مَفْعُولٌ.","urdu":"اسم فاعل کام کرنے والا، اسم مفعول جس پر کام ہوا۔","examples":[{"ar":"كَتَبَ","label":"Root verb","note":"Identify the original radicals before deriving a noun."},{"ar":"كَاتِبٌ","label":"Writer","note":"Place the three radicals into فَاعِلٌ to name the doer: a writer."},{"ar":"مَكْتُوبٌ","label":"Written","note":"Place them into مَفْعُولٌ to name what received the action: something written."}],"question":"Which is the passive participle?","correct":"مَكْتُوبٌ","options":["مَكْتُوبٌ","كَاتِبٌ","كِتَابَةٌ"],"feedback":"The regular pattern is مَفْعُولٌ.","mode":"people"},{"id":16,"book":"S1","pages":"31-32","section":"25–26","ar":"اسْمُ الظَّرْفِ وَالْآلَةِ وَالتَّفْضِيلِ","title":"Place, instrument & comparison","rule":"Derived nouns can identify time/place, an instrument or a comparison. Learn the pattern and actual lexical usage together.","urdu":"اسم ظرف، اسم آلہ اور اسم تفضیل۔","examples":[{"ar":"مَجْلِسٌ","label":"Place of sitting","note":"مَجْلِسٌ names a place of sitting, from the root ج ل س."},{"ar":"مِفْتَاحٌ","label":"Key/instrument","note":"مِفْتَاحٌ names an instrument used to open something, from ف ت ح."},{"ar":"أَكْبَرُ","label":"Greater","note":"أَكْبَرُ expresses comparison on the pattern أَفْعَلُ."}],"question":"Which denotes an instrument?","correct":"مِفْتَاحٌ","options":["مِفْتَاحٌ","مَجْلِسٌ","أَكْبَرُ"],"feedback":"A key is the instrument used to open.","mode":"cards"},{"id":17,"book":"S1","pages":"33-35","section":"27–29","ar":"الْوَزْنُ وَالْأَبْوَابُ","title":"Patterns and bāb","rule":"Match the three root letters to ف، ع، ل. Additional letters are not part of the base root. Compare a verb’s past and present vowel patterns.","urdu":"حروف اصلی اور زائد، وزن اور باب کی پہچان۔","examples":[{"ar":"نَصَرَ","label":"فَعَلَ","note":"Map ن ص ر to ف ع ل: the past middle vowel is fatḥah."},{"ar":"يَنْصُرُ","label":"يَفْعُلُ","note":"The present middle vowel is ḍammah, giving the familiar نصر family."},{"ar":"ضَرَبَ","label":"فَعَلَ","note":"Map ض ر ب to the same past pattern فَعَلَ."},{"ar":"يَضْرِبُ","label":"يَفْعِلُ","note":"The present middle vowel is kasrah, so it belongs to a different bāb despite the same past pattern."}],"question":"Weight of نَصَرَ?","correct":"فَعَلَ","options":["فَعَلَ","فَاعَلَ","أَفْعَلَ"],"feedback":"All three letters are radicals.","mode":"flow"},{"id":18,"book":"S1","pages":"36-40","section":"30–35","ar":"الْمُجَرَّدُ وَالْمَزِيدُ","title":"Verb families & seven types","rule":"Distinguish bare and augmented stems, then classify roots by their radical properties; use the book’s lists of abwāb and haft aqsām.","urdu":"مجرد و مزید اور ہفت اقسام کا تعارف۔","examples":[{"ar":"فَتَحَ","label":"Bare","note":"فَتَحَ has three original consonants and no augmented letter."},{"ar":"أَكْرَمَ","label":"Augmented pattern","note":"أَكْرَمَ adds an initial hamzah to the root ك ر م; it illustrates the pattern أَفْعَلَ."},{"ar":"فَتَّحَ","label":"Augmented","note":"فَتَّحَ doubles the middle radical in the augmented pattern فَعَّلَ."},{"ar":"وَعَدَ","label":"Weak first radical","note":"وَعَدَ has a weak initial radical, so it is mithāl."},{"ar":"مَدَّ","label":"Doubled","note":"مَدَّ has two identical final radicals; the shaddah represents them together."}],"question":"Which has a doubled root radical?","correct":"مَدَّ","options":["مَدَّ","وَعَدَ","فَتَحَ"],"feedback":"The second and third radicals of مَدَّ are both د.","mode":"people"},{"id":19,"book":"S2","pages":"3-5","section":"Opening","ar":"الْمَادَّةُ وَالْوَزْنُ","title":"Terminology review","rule":"Identify root, pattern, transitivity, voice, and conjugation. A lāzim verb does not require a direct object; a mutaʿaddī verb takes one.","urdu":"فعل لازم، متعدی، مادہ، وزن اور بحث۔","examples":[{"ar":"جَلَسَ","label":"Intransitive","note":"جَلَسَ is complete with its doer: someone sat. It does not need a direct object."},{"ar":"كَتَبَ","label":"Transitive in context","note":"كَتَبَ can take a direct object, as in writing a lesson."},{"ar":"مَكْتُوبٌ","label":"Derived","note":"مَكْتُوبٌ is a derived noun identifying something written."},{"ar":"ك ت ب","label":"Root","note":"The root is the set of original radicals shared by related words."}],"question":"Which is intransitive?","correct":"جَلَسَ","options":["جَلَسَ","ضَرَبَ","نَصَرَ"],"feedback":"Sitting does not require a direct object.","mode":"people"},{"id":20,"book":"S2","pages":"6-10","section":"Past","ar":"الْمَاضِي","title":"Past constructions in depth","rule":"The book distinguishes مطلق، قریب، بعید، استمراری، احتمالی، تمنائی. Study the active/passive and affirmative/negative combinations.","urdu":"ماضی کی مختلف قسمیں اور ان کی گردانیں۔","examples":[{"ar":"كَتَبَ","label":"Simple past","note":"The simple past is the base from which several constructions are compared."},{"ar":"قَدْ كَتَبَ","label":"Near past","note":"قَدْ gives the past action a completed or near-past interpretation in context."},{"ar":"كَانَ يَكْتُبُ","label":"Continuous","note":"كَانَ plus the present gives a continuing or habitual past action."},{"ar":"مَا كَتَبَ","label":"Negative","note":"مَا denies the action without changing the base past conjugation."},{"ar":"كَانَ قَدْ كَتَبَ","label":"Remote past","note":"He had written. The book distinguishes remote past from simple and recent past."},{"ar":"لَعَلَّهُ كَتَبَ","label":"Probable past","note":"Perhaps he wrote; لَعَلَّ expresses possibility or expectation."},{"ar":"لَيْتَهُ كَتَبَ","label":"Wished-for past","note":"If only he had written; لَيْتَ expresses wishing."}],"question":"Which is negative past?","correct":"مَا كَتَبَ","options":["مَا كَتَبَ","قَدْ كَتَبَ","كَتَبَ"],"feedback":"Mā negates the past.","mode":"timeline"},{"id":21,"book":"S2","pages":"11-13","section":"Present","ar":"الْمُضَارِعُ","title":"Present constructions","rule":"The present can indicate present or future. سَ and سَوْفَ mark future; لَا negates without imposing jazm when used as ordinary negation.","urdu":"مضارع میں حال و استقبال اور نفی کی مشق۔","examples":[{"ar":"يَذْهَبُ","label":"He goes","note":"The present form can refer to now or the future depending on context."},{"ar":"سَيَذْهَبُ","label":"He will go","note":"سَ joins the front of the present verb and indicates the future."},{"ar":"سَوْفَ يَذْهَبُ","label":"He will go","note":"سَوْفَ is a separate future particle before the present verb."},{"ar":"لَا يَذْهَبُ","label":"He does not go","note":"Ordinary لَا negates the present without the jussive effect of prohibitive لَا."}],"question":"Which has a future particle?","correct":"سَيَذْهَبُ","options":["سَيَذْهَبُ","ذَهَبَ","لَا يَذْهَبُ"],"feedback":"Sīn indicates future.","mode":"timeline"},{"id":22,"book":"S2","pages":"13-20","section":"Negation","ar":"النَّفْيُ وَالتَّأْكِيدُ","title":"Negation and emphasis","rule":"Contrast لَنْ with لَمْ and use the heavy/light nūn tables for emphasis. Observe the vowel and suffix changes in the original tables.","urdu":"نفی تاکید بلن، نفی بلم اور نون تاکید۔","examples":[{"ar":"يَفْعَلُ","label":"Indicative","note":"The regular singular indicative form ends in ḍammah."},{"ar":"لَنْ يَفْعَلَ","label":"Subjunctive","note":"After لَنْ, the ending becomes fatḥah, the sign of naṣb here."},{"ar":"لَمْ يَفْعَلْ","label":"Jussive","note":"After لَمْ, the ending becomes sukūn, the sign of jazm here."},{"ar":"لَيَفْعَلَنَّ","label":"Emphasis","note":"The heavy nūn emphasizes the verb; this is a separate construction from ordinary mood government."}],"question":"Which is governed by لَنْ?","correct":"يَفْعَلَ","options":["يَفْعَلَ","يَفْعَلُ","يَفْعَلْ"],"feedback":"Lan governs naṣb.","mode":"timeline"},{"id":23,"book":"S2","pages":"20-23","section":"Amr","ar":"فِعْلُ الْأَمْرِ","title":"Command patterns","rule":"Use the second-person jussive to construct direct active imperatives; use the book’s tables for the other command forms and emphasis.","urdu":"امر کی گردانوں اور علامات کی مشق۔","examples":[{"ar":"تَنْصُرُ","label":"Present","note":"Begin with the second-person singular present form."},{"ar":"تَنْصُرْ","label":"Jussive","note":"Apply jazm: the last radical becomes sākin."},{"ar":"نْصُرْ","label":"Remove prefix","note":"Remove the prefix تَ, leaving a cluster beginning with sukūn."},{"ar":"اُنْصُرْ","label":"Command","note":"Supply hamzat al-waṣl with ḍammah for this stem to produce the command."}],"question":"Dual command for two people?","correct":"اُنْصُرَا","options":["اُنْصُرَا","اُنْصُرُوا","اُنْصُرْ"],"feedback":"Dual commands end in alif.","mode":"flow"},{"id":24,"book":"S2","pages":"23-26","section":"Nahy","ar":"فِعْلُ النَّهْيِ","title":"Prohibition patterns","rule":"Use lā of prohibition with a jussive present. In the five indicative verb patterns that end with nūn, jazm drops that nūn.","urdu":"نہی کی گردان میں نون اعرابی کے حذف کو دیکھیں۔","examples":[{"ar":"تَكْتُبُونَ","label":"Indicative","note":"The masculine plural present has the indicative nūn."},{"ar":"تَكْتُبُوا","label":"Jussive","note":"Jazm removes the nūn while preserving the plural wāw."},{"ar":"لَا تَكْتُبُوا","label":"Do not write (plural)","note":"Add prohibitive لَا to form the plural prohibition."}],"question":"Plural prohibition?","correct":"لَا تَكْتُبُوا","options":["لَا تَكْتُبُوا","لَا تَكْتُبُونَ","اُكْتُبُوا"],"feedback":"The indicative nūn is removed.","mode":"flow"},{"id":25,"book":"S2","pages":"27-30","section":"Derived nouns","ar":"الْمُشْتَقَّاتُ","title":"Active & passive participles","rule":"Form I uses فَاعِلٌ and مَفْعُولٌ for regular roots. In augmented stems, the present prefix is replaced by م with ḍammah; the penultimate vowel distinguishes active and passive.","urdu":"اسم مشتق، اسم فاعل اور اسم مفعول۔","examples":[{"ar":"أَكْرَمَ","label":"Form IV","note":"Identify the augmented past stem أَكْرَمَ."},{"ar":"يُكْرِمُ","label":"Present","note":"Its present form provides the basis for the derived participles."},{"ar":"مُكْرِمٌ","label":"Active participle","note":"Replace the present prefix with مُـ and retain kasrah before the last radical for the active participle."},{"ar":"مُكْرَمٌ","label":"Passive participle","note":"Use fatḥah before the final radical for the passive participle."}],"question":"Which is active?","correct":"مُكْرِمٌ","options":["مُكْرِمٌ","مُكْرَمٌ","إِكْرَامٌ"],"feedback":"Kasrah before the last radical marks active here.","mode":"flow"},{"id":26,"book":"S2","pages":"30-34","section":"Nouns","ar":"الظَّرْفُ وَالْآلَةُ وَالتَّفْضِيلُ","title":"Place, tool, comparison","rule":"Learn the patterns of place/time, instrument and comparative nouns, and consult lexical examples before applying a pattern.","urdu":"اسم ظرف، اسم آلہ اور اسم تفضیل کے اوزان۔","examples":[{"ar":"مَكْتَبٌ","label":"Place","note":"مَكْتَبٌ denotes a place associated with writing."},{"ar":"مِفْتَاحٌ","label":"Instrument","note":"مِفْتَاحٌ denotes a tool used for opening."},{"ar":"أَحْسَنُ","label":"Better","note":"أَحْسَنُ compares goodness or beauty."}],"question":"Which is comparative?","correct":"أَحْسَنُ","options":["أَحْسَنُ","مَكْتَبٌ","مِفْتَاحٌ"],"feedback":"أَفْعَلُ is a common comparative pattern.","mode":"cards"},{"id":27,"book":"S2","pages":"34-38","section":"Qualities","ar":"الْمُبَالَغَةُ وَالصِّفَةُ وَالْمَصْدَرُ","title":"Intensive forms, qualities, verbal nouns","rule":"An intensive noun strengthens a quality or action; a ṣifah mushabbahah commonly expresses a stable quality; a maṣdar names the action without tense.","urdu":"اسم مبالغہ، صفت مشبہہ اور مصدر۔","examples":[{"ar":"غَفَّارٌ","label":"Intensive","note":"غَفَّارٌ expresses intensive or repeated forgiveness."},{"ar":"حَسَنٌ","label":"Quality","note":"حَسَنٌ expresses a characteristic or quality."},{"ar":"كِتَابَةٌ","label":"Verbal noun","note":"كِتَابَةٌ names the act of writing without locating it in time."}],"question":"Which names an action without tense?","correct":"كِتَابَةٌ","options":["كِتَابَةٌ","كَاتِبٌ","كَتَبَ"],"feedback":"A maṣdar does not specify tense.","mode":"cards"},{"id":28,"book":"S2","pages":"39-45","section":"Abwab","ar":"الثُّلَاثِيُّ الْمُجَرَّدُ","title":"Six triliteral verb families","rule":"The six familiar sound-form patterns pair past and present vowels. The book discusses five together and the remaining pattern separately; memorize the pair with each verb.","urdu":"ثلاثی مجرد کے ابواب، ماضی و مضارع کے اوزان۔","examples":[{"ar":"نَصَرَ يَنْصُرُ","label":"a/u","note":"Past fatḥah, present ḍammah: بَابُ نَصَرَ."},{"ar":"ضَرَبَ يَضْرِبُ","label":"a/i","note":"Past fatḥah, present kasrah: بَابُ ضَرَبَ."},{"ar":"فَتَحَ يَفْتَحُ","label":"a/a","note":"Past fatḥah, present fatḥah: بَابُ فَتَحَ."},{"ar":"سَمِعَ يَسْمَعُ","label":"i/a","note":"Past kasrah, present fatḥah: بَابُ سَمِعَ."},{"ar":"كَرُمَ يَكْرُمُ","label":"u/u","note":"Past ḍammah, present ḍammah: بَابُ كَرُمَ."},{"ar":"حَسِبَ يَحْسِبُ","label":"i/i","note":"Past kasrah, present kasrah: بَابُ حَسِبَ."}],"question":"Which is باب نصر?","correct":"نَصَرَ يَنْصُرُ","options":["نَصَرَ يَنْصُرُ","ضَرَبَ يَضْرِبُ","سَمِعَ يَسْمَعُ"],"feedback":"The pair is فَعَلَ يَفْعُلُ.","mode":"cards"},{"id":29,"book":"S2","pages":"45-53","section":"Augmented","ar":"الثُّلَاثِيُّ الْمَزِيدُ","title":"Augmented triliteral patterns","rule":"Additional letters create new stems. Compare the book’s groups by resemblance to quadriliteral patterns and non-attached forms; meanings vary by verb.","urdu":"ثلاثی مزید فیہ کے ابواب اور مصادر۔","examples":[{"ar":"عَلِمَ","label":"Base","note":"The base root ع ل م expresses knowledge."},{"ar":"عَلَّمَ","label":"Form II","note":"Doubling the middle radical gives عَلَّمَ, to teach."},{"ar":"أَعْلَمَ","label":"Form IV","note":"The initial hamzah gives أَعْلَمَ, to inform."},{"ar":"تَعَلَّمَ","label":"Form V","note":"تَعَلَّمَ is a further derived form, to learn."},{"ar":"اِسْتَعْلَمَ","label":"Form X","note":"اِسْتَعْلَمَ uses the pattern اِسْتَفْعَلَ, here to inquire."},{"ar":"قَاتَلَ يُقَاتِلُ","label":"Form III: فَاعَلَ","note":"An alif follows the first radical. The form often denotes interaction, but its meaning depends on the verb."},{"ar":"اِنْكَسَرَ يَنْكَسِرُ","label":"Form VII: اِنْفَعَلَ","note":"The prefix انـ is added. Here the meaning is that something broke or became broken."},{"ar":"اِجْتَمَعَ يَجْتَمِعُ","label":"Form VIII: اِفْتَعَلَ","note":"An initial connecting hamzah and a tāʾ after the first radical form the pattern. Here: he gathered."},{"ar":"اِحْمَرَّ يَحْمَرُّ","label":"Form IX: اِفْعَلَّ","note":"An initial connecting hamzah and doubling of the third radical occur. Often used for colours or physical qualities."},{"ar":"تَعَاوَنَ يَتَعَاوَنُ","label":"Form VI: تَفَاعَلَ","note":"A tāʾ at the start and alif after the first radical. Here: he cooperated."},{"ar":"اِسْتَغْفَرَ يَسْتَغْفِرُ","label":"Form X: اِسْتَفْعَلَ","note":"استـ precedes the root. Here the meaning is seeking forgiveness, not a universal meaning for every Form X verb."}],"question":"Which matches اسْتَفْعَلَ?","correct":"اِسْتَعْلَمَ","options":["اِسْتَعْلَمَ","عَلَّمَ","تَعَلَّمَ"],"feedback":"The prefix است is characteristic of this pattern.","mode":"flow"},{"id":30,"book":"S2","pages":"53-62","section":"Quadriliteral","ar":"الرُّبَاعِيُّ وَالْمُلْحَقُ","title":"Four-radical & attached forms","rule":"A bare quadriliteral has four original letters; other patterns are augmented or attached by analogy. Follow the book’s separate lists.","urdu":"رباعی مجرد، مزید اور ملحق کے ابواب۔","examples":[{"ar":"دَحْرَجَ","label":"Bare quadriliteral","note":"All four consonants of دَحْرَجَ are original."},{"ar":"يُدَحْرِجُ","label":"Present","note":"The present form changes the affixes and vowels but retains four radicals."},{"ar":"تَدَحْرَجَ","label":"Augmented quadriliteral","note":"تَدَحْرَجَ adds تـ to the quadriliteral base."}],"question":"Which is bare quadriliteral?","correct":"دَحْرَجَ","options":["دَحْرَجَ","نَصَرَ","أَكْرَمَ"],"feedback":"All four letters in دحرج are original.","mode":"flow"},{"id":31,"book":"S2","pages":"62-65","section":"Root recognition","ar":"هَفْتْ أَقْسَامٌ","title":"Seven root types & finding the original","rule":"Classify by the presence of hamzah, repeated radicals, or weak letters. Work back from a form to its root using pattern and sound-change clues; the book does not replace dictionary verification.","urdu":"ہفت اقسام اور کسی اصل کو معلوم کرنے کا طریقہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"كَتَبَ","label":"Sound","note":"The three radicals are sound and distinct."},{"ar":"أَخَذَ","label":"Hamzated","note":"The first radical is hamzah, so the root is hamzated."},{"ar":"مَدَّ","label":"Doubled","note":"The second and third radicals are identical: م د د."},{"ar":"وَعَدَ","label":"Mithāl","note":"The first radical is weak: و ع د."},{"ar":"قَالَ","label":"Ajwaf","note":"The middle radical is weak: ق و ل."},{"ar":"رَمَى","label":"Nāqiṣ","note":"The final radical is weak: ر م ي."},{"ar":"وَقَى","label":"Lafīf","note":"Two radicals are weak: و ق ي."},{"ar":"وَفَى","label":"Lafīf mafrūq","note":"Two weak radicals separated by a sound radical: و ف ي. The first and third radicals are weak."},{"ar":"طَوَى","label":"Lafīf maqrūn","note":"Two adjacent weak radicals: ط و ي. The second and third radicals are weak."}],"question":"Which is أجوف?","correct":"قَالَ","options":["قَالَ","وَعَدَ","رَمَى"],"feedback":"Its middle radical is weak (و).","mode":"people"},{"id":32,"book":"N1","pages":"3-4","section":"1–2","ar":"الْكَلِمَةُ","title":"Word classification","rule":"Nahw studies how words combine and how their final endings behave. A meaningful individual word can be an ism, fiʿl or ḥarf. Learn the identifying signs.","urdu":"اسم، فعل اور حرف کی علامات۔","examples":[{"ar":"الْكِتَابُ","label":"Ism","note":"الـ identifies a noun; الْكِتَابُ names a thing."},{"ar":"كَتَبَ","label":"Fiʿl","note":"كَتَبَ denotes an action connected to a time."},{"ar":"فِي","label":"Ḥarf","note":"فِي is a particle; its full relational meaning emerges with another word."}],"question":"Which is a particle?","correct":"فِي","options":["فِي","كَتَبَ","الْكِتَابُ"],"feedback":"Its meaning is completed in connection with other words.","mode":"roles"},{"id":33,"book":"N1","pages":"5","section":"3","ar":"الْمُرَكَّبُ","title":"Phrases and sentences","rule":"A useful complete construction conveys a complete meaning; an incomplete phrase needs more context. A nominal sentence begins with a nominal constituent; a verbal sentence with a verb, subject to detailed rules.","urdu":"مرکب مفید و غیر مفید، جملہ اسمیہ و فعلیہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"كِتَابُ زَيْدٍ","label":"Incomplete phrase","note":"The two nouns form a possessive phrase: Zayd's book. No complete assertion is made."},{"ar":"زَيْدٌ قَائِمٌ","label":"Nominal sentence","note":"The subject زَيْدٌ and predicate قَائِمٌ form a complete nominal statement."},{"ar":"قَامَ زَيْدٌ","label":"Verbal sentence","note":"The verb قَامَ and its subject زَيْدٌ form a complete verbal statement."}],"question":"Which is a verbal sentence?","correct":"قَامَ زَيْدٌ","options":["قَامَ زَيْدٌ","زَيْدٌ قَائِمٌ","كِتَابُ زَيْدٍ"],"feedback":"It begins with a verb.","mode":"roles"},{"id":34,"book":"N1","pages":"6-7","section":"4–5","ar":"الْعَدَدُ وَالْجِنْسُ","title":"Number and gender","rule":"Nouns may be singular, dual or plural. Plurals can be sound or broken. Gender may be masculine or feminine, including feminine forms without overt markers.","urdu":"واحد، تثنیہ، جمع؛ مذکر و مؤنث۔","examples":[{"ar":"رَجُلٌ","label":"Singular","note":"One man: the singular."},{"ar":"رَجُلَانِ","label":"Dual","note":"Two men: the dual, marked here by ـَانِ in rafʿ."},{"ar":"رِجَالٌ","label":"Broken plural","note":"The broken plural changes the internal shape of the singular."},{"ar":"مُسْلِمَاتٌ","label":"Sound feminine plural","note":"The sound feminine plural uses the ـَات suffix."},{"ar":"مُسْلِمُونَ / مُسْلِمِينَ","label":"Sound masculine plural","note":"Wāw plus nūn marks nominative; yāʾ plus nūn marks accusative/genitive. The ending nūn drops in iḍāfah."},{"ar":"مُسْلِمَاتٌ / مُسْلِمَاتٍ","label":"Sound feminine plural","note":"Nominative has ḍammah; accusative and genitive have kasrah in this plural."}],"question":"Which is a dual?","correct":"رَجُلَانِ","options":["رَجُلَانِ","رِجَالٌ","رَجُلٌ"],"feedback":"The nominative dual ending is انِ.","mode":"roles"},{"id":35,"book":"N1","pages":"8","section":"6","ar":"الْمَعْرِفَةُ وَالنَّكِرَةُ","title":"Definite and indefinite","rule":"A nakirah refers to an unspecified entity. A maʿrifah refers to a specified entity, including pronouns, names and nouns made definite by ال or appropriate iḍāfah.","urdu":"معرفہ اور نکرہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"كِتَابٌ","label":"Indefinite","note":"The indefinite كِتَابٌ can refer to any book."},{"ar":"الْكِتَابُ","label":"Definite","note":"الـ makes this noun definite: the book."},{"ar":"زَيْدٌ","label":"Proper name","note":"A proper name such as زَيْدٌ is definite even though it can take tanwīn."},{"ar":"هَذَا / أَنَا / الَّذِي","label":"Other definite classes","note":"Demonstratives, personal pronouns and relative nouns are definite."},{"ar":"كِتَابُ زَيْدٍ","label":"Definite through iḍāfah","note":"A noun can become definite through possessive construction with a definite noun."}],"question":"Which is indefinite?","correct":"كِتَابٌ","options":["كِتَابٌ","الْكِتَابُ","زَيْدٌ"],"feedback":"It denotes an unspecified book.","mode":"roles"},{"id":36,"book":"N1","pages":"9","section":"7","ar":"الْمُعْرَبُ وَالْمَبْنِيُّ","title":"Inflection and fixed endings","rule":"A muʿrab word changes its ending because of grammatical governance; a mabnī word maintains its ending despite a change in role.","urdu":"معرب کا آخر عامل سے بدلتا ہے؛ مبنی کا آخر برقرار رہتا ہے۔","examples":[{"ar":"جَاءَ زَيْدٌ","label":"Rafʿ","note":"As subject of جَاءَ, Zayd is marfūʿ with ḍammah."},{"ar":"رَأَيْتُ زَيْدًا","label":"Naṣb","note":"As direct object of رَأَيْتُ, Zayd is manṣūb with fatḥah."},{"ar":"مَرَرْتُ بِزَيْدٍ","label":"Jarr","note":"After بِـ, Zayd is majrūr with kasrah. The word remains the same, but its grammatical ending changes."}],"question":"Which is accusative?","correct":"زَيْدًا","options":["زَيْدًا","زَيْدٌ","زَيْدٍ"],"feedback":"The direct object takes naṣb.","mode":"grammar"},{"id":37,"book":"N1","pages":"10-13","section":"8–10","ar":"الضَّمِيرُ وَالْإِشَارَةُ وَالْمَوْصُولُ","title":"Pronouns, demonstratives, relatives","rule":"Distinguish attached/detached pronouns, demonstratives, and relative nouns. A relative noun needs its connecting clause (ṣilah).","urdu":"ضمیر، اسم اشارہ اور اسم موصول۔","examples":[{"ar":"هُوَ","label":"Detached pronoun","note":"هُوَ stands independently as a detached pronoun."},{"ar":"كِتَابُهُ","label":"Attached pronoun","note":"The suffix ـهُ attaches to كِتَابُ and indicates his book."},{"ar":"هَذَا","label":"Demonstrative","note":"هَذَا points to a nearby masculine singular entity."},{"ar":"الَّذِي","label":"Relative","note":"الَّذِي introduces a relative clause that identifies its referent."},{"ar":"إِيَّاكَ","label":"Detached accusative pronoun","note":"A detached object pronoun, used here for a masculine singular addressee."},{"ar":"كَتَبْتُهُ","label":"Attached object pronoun","note":"The attached تُ is the subject; the attached هُ is the object."},{"ar":"هَذَانِ / هَاتَانِ","label":"Dual demonstratives","note":"Masculine and feminine dual proximal forms in nominative; in accusative/genitive they are هَذَيْنِ and هَاتَيْنِ."},{"ar":"ذَلِكَ / تِلْكَ","label":"Distant demonstratives","note":"Masculine and feminine singular demonstratives for a distant referent."},{"ar":"الَّتِي / الَّذِينَ / اللَّاتِي","label":"Relative nouns","note":"Feminine singular, masculine plural and feminine plural relative forms; each requires a relative clause."}],"question":"Which is a relative noun?","correct":"الَّذِي","options":["الَّذِي","هَذَا","هُوَ"],"feedback":"It introduces a relative clause.","mode":"roles"},{"id":38,"book":"N1","pages":"13-15","section":"11","ar":"الْمُنْصَرِفُ وَغَيْرُ الْمُنْصَرِفِ","title":"Triptotes and diptotes","rule":"A diptote ordinarily has no tanwīn and uses fatḥah for jarr when neither definite with ال nor muḍāf. The book gives causes for this restriction.","urdu":"غیر منصرف میں تنوین نہیں؛ بعض حالتوں میں جر کی علامت زبر ہے۔","examples":[{"ar":"زَيْدٌ","label":"Triptote","note":"زَيْدٌ is a fully declinable proper name and can carry tanwīn."},{"ar":"أَحْمَدُ","label":"Diptote","note":"أَحْمَدُ is a diptote, with no tanwīn in this form."},{"ar":"بِأَحْمَدَ","label":"Jarr with fatḥah","note":"After بِـ, the diptote أَحْمَدَ uses fatḥah for jarr because it has neither الـ nor iḍāfah."}],"question":"Which is the ordinary indefinite diptote genitive form here?","correct":"بِأَحْمَدَ","options":["بِأَحْمَدَ","بِأَحْمَدٍ","بِأَحْمَدُ"],"feedback":"Diptote jarr uses fatḥah in this context.","mode":"roles"},{"id":39,"book":"N1","pages":"15-17","section":"12–13","ar":"الْمَرْفُوعَاتُ","title":"Nominative roles","rule":"Identify the fāʿil, nāʾib al-fāʿil, mubtadaʾ and khabar as nominative roles. Their visible case marks depend on noun type.","urdu":"فاعل، نائب فاعل، مبتدا اور خبر۔","examples":[{"ar":"كَتَبَ زَيْدٌ","label":"Fāʿil","note":"زَيْدٌ is the doer of the active verb, so it is raised."},{"ar":"كُتِبَ الدَّرْسُ","label":"Nāʾib fāʿil","note":"الدَّرْسُ becomes the raised deputy subject of the passive verb."},{"ar":"زَيْدٌ قَائِمٌ","label":"Mubtadaʾ and khabar","note":"In a basic nominal sentence both the subject and predicate are raised."}],"question":"In كُتِبَ الدَّرْسُ what is الدَّرْسُ?","correct":"نَائِبُ الْفَاعِلِ","options":["نَائِبُ الْفَاعِلِ","مَفْعُولٌ بِهِ","مُضَافٌ إِلَيْهِ"],"feedback":"A passive verb raises its deputy subject.","mode":"roles"},{"id":40,"book":"N1","pages":"17-19","section":"14–15","ar":"إِنَّ وَكَانَ","title":"Governing particles & incomplete verbs","rule":"Inna and sisters govern the ism in naṣb and khabar in rafʿ; kāna and sisters govern the ism in rafʿ and khabar in naṣb.","urdu":"حروف مشبہ بالفعل اور افعال ناقصہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"زَيْدٌ قَائِمٌ","label":"Base","note":"Without a governing expression, both the subject and predicate carry rafʿ."},{"ar":"إِنَّ زَيْدًا قَائِمٌ","label":"Inna","note":"إِنَّ makes its noun زَيْدًا manṣūb; the predicate stays marfūʿ."},{"ar":"كَانَ زَيْدٌ قَائِمًا","label":"Kāna","note":"كَانَ keeps its noun زَيْدٌ marfūʿ but makes قَائِمًا manṣūb."}],"question":"Which is the khabar of kāna?","correct":"قَائِمًا","options":["قَائِمًا","زَيْدٌ","كَانَ"],"feedback":"Kāna places its predicate in naṣb.","mode":"grammar"},{"id":41,"book":"N1","pages":"19-21","section":"16–17","ar":"الْمَفَاعِيلُ","title":"Objects and accusatives","rule":"Separate direct object, cognate object, purpose, accompaniment, and time/place expressions; each has its own semantic relationship with the verb.","urdu":"مفعول بہ، مطلق، لہ، معہ اور فیہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"كَتَبَ زَيْدٌ دَرْسًا","label":"Direct object","note":"دَرْسًا receives the action of writing, so it is the direct object."},{"ar":"ضَرَبْتُ ضَرْبًا","label":"Cognate object","note":"ضَرْبًا is a verbal noun of the same action and reinforces the striking."},{"ar":"سَافَرْتُ لَيْلًا","label":"Time","note":"لَيْلًا tells when the travel took place; it is an adverb of time."},{"ar":"قُمْتُ إِكْرَامًا لِلضَّيْفِ","label":"Object of purpose","note":"إِكْرَامًا explains why I stood up; it is the accusative object of purpose."},{"ar":"سِرْتُ وَالنَّهْرَ","label":"Object of accompaniment","note":"النَّهْرَ follows the wāw of accompaniment: I walked alongside the river."},{"ar":"جَلَسْتُ أَمَامَ الْبَيْتِ","label":"Adverb of place","note":"أَمَامَ is accusative and specifies where I sat. الْبَيْتِ is its genitive dependent."}],"question":"What is ضَرْبًا in ضَرَبْتُ ضَرْبًا?","correct":"مَفْعُولٌ مُطْلَقٌ","options":["مَفْعُولٌ مُطْلَقٌ","فَاعِلٌ","مُبْتَدَأٌ"],"feedback":"The maṣdar reinforces or specifies the action.","mode":"roles"},{"id":42,"book":"N1","pages":"21-23","section":"18–19","ar":"الْحَالُ وَالتَّمْيِيزُ وَالِاسْتِثْنَاءُ","title":"State, specification, exception","rule":"Ḥāl describes a participant’s state; tamyīz removes ambiguity; exception excludes an item from a group. Each has its own rules.","urdu":"حال، تمییز اور مستثنیٰ۔","examples":[{"ar":"جَاءَ زَيْدٌ رَاكِبًا","label":"State","note":"رَاكِبًا describes the state of Zayd when he arrived."},{"ar":"عِنْدِي عِشْرُونَ كِتَابًا","label":"Specification","note":"كِتَابًا clarifies what the number twenty counts."},{"ar":"جَاءَ الْقَوْمُ إِلَّا زَيْدًا","label":"Exception","note":"زَيْدًا is excluded from the group in this complete affirmative exception."}],"question":"Which word is the ḥāl in the first example?","correct":"رَاكِبًا","options":["رَاكِبًا","زَيْدٌ","جَاءَ"],"feedback":"It tells the state in which Zayd arrived.","mode":"roles"},{"id":43,"book":"N1","pages":"23-24","section":"20","ar":"الْمَجْرُورَاتُ","title":"Genitive constructions","rule":"A noun is governed in jarr by a preposition or by iḍāfah. The muḍāf does not take tanwīn; the muḍāf ilayh is majrūr.","urdu":"مجرورات: حرف جر اور مضاف الیہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"فِي الْبَيْتِ","label":"Preposition","note":"فِي governs الْبَيْتِ in jarr, indicated by kasrah."},{"ar":"كِتَابُ زَيْدٍ","label":"Iḍāfah","note":"Zayd is the possessor in a genitive construction; the second noun is majrūr."},{"ar":"كِتَابُ","label":"Muḍāf","note":"The muḍāf does not take tanwīn and obtains its case from its position in the larger sentence."},{"ar":"زَيْدٍ","label":"Muḍāf ilayh","note":"The muḍāf ilayh is genitive regardless of the first noun's case."}],"question":"What is زَيْدٍ in كِتَابُ زَيْدٍ?","correct":"مُضَافٌ إِلَيْهِ","options":["مُضَافٌ إِلَيْهِ","فَاعِلٌ","خَبَرٌ"],"feedback":"The second term of iḍāfah is majrūr.","mode":"roles"},{"id":44,"book":"N1","pages":"24-28","section":"21–23","ar":"التَّوَابِعُ","title":"Dependent nouns","rule":"A dependent follows the case of its governing antecedent. Distinguish adjective, emphasis, apposition and conjunction; adjective agreement has additional conditions.","urdu":"صفت، تاکید، بدل اور عطف۔","examples":[{"ar":"جَاءَ رَجُلٌ صَالِحٌ","label":"Adjective","note":"صَالِحٌ describes رَجُلٌ and follows its case and indefiniteness."},{"ar":"جَاءَ زَيْدٌ نَفْسُهُ","label":"Emphasis","note":"نَفْسُهُ emphasizes that Zayd himself came."},{"ar":"جَاءَ زَيْدٌ وَعَمْرٌو","label":"Conjunction","note":"وَ joins عَمْرٌو to the preceding raised noun; the second noun shares its case."},{"ar":"جَاءَ أَخُوكَ زَيْدٌ","label":"Apposition (badal)","note":"زَيْدٌ clarifies the identity of أَخُوكَ and follows its nominative case."},{"ar":"مَرَرْتُ بِرَجُلٍ صَالِحٍ","label":"Adjective in genitive","note":"صَالِحٍ follows رَجُلٍ in case and indefiniteness."}],"question":"What is صَالِحٌ in the first example?","correct":"صِفَةٌ","options":["صِفَةٌ","مُضَافٌ إِلَيْهِ","مَفْعُولٌ بِهِ"],"feedback":"It describes رَجُلٌ and agrees with it.","mode":"roles"},{"id":45,"book":"N1","pages":"28-34","section":"24–29","ar":"أَقْسَامُ الْفِعْلِ وَالْعَمَلُ","title":"Verbs and governing derivatives","rule":"Study transitive and intransitive verbs, active and passive, and the grammatical work of maṣdar and participles; their governance depends on conditions.","urdu":"فعل کی اقسام اور اسم فاعل و مفعول وغیرہ کا عمل۔","examples":[{"ar":"جَلَسَ زَيْدٌ","label":"Intransitive","note":"جَلَسَ needs a subject but no direct object."},{"ar":"كَتَبَ زَيْدٌ دَرْسًا","label":"Transitive","note":"كَتَبَ in this example has a subject and a direct object."},{"ar":"كُتِبَ الدَّرْسُ","label":"Passive","note":"In the passive, the lesson is now the raised deputy subject rather than an accusative direct object."}],"question":"Which verb takes a direct object in the examples?","correct":"كَتَبَ","options":["كَتَبَ","جَلَسَ","كُتِبَ"],"feedback":"It governs دَرْسًا as its direct object.","mode":"roles"},{"id":46,"book":"N1","pages":"35-38","section":"30–33","ar":"الْحُرُوفُ","title":"Particles and conditional forms","rule":"Recognize governing and non-governing particles. Distinguish interrogation, negation, calling, conjunction and condition; check the verb’s resulting case or mood.","urdu":"حروف عاملہ و غیر عاملہ، نفی، ندا اور شرط۔","examples":[{"ar":"هَلْ","label":"Question","note":"هَلْ introduces a yes-or-no question."},{"ar":"لَمْ يَذْهَبْ","label":"Jussive","note":"لَمْ denies a past event and makes the following present verb jussive."},{"ar":"يَا زَيْدُ","label":"Call","note":"يَا calls someone; زَيْدُ is a single proper name in the vocative construction."},{"ar":"إِنْ تَدْرُسْ تَنْجَحْ","label":"Condition","note":"إِنْ introduces a condition; both regular present verbs here have jussive endings."}],"question":"Which particle governs jazm in the second example?","correct":"لَمْ","options":["لَمْ","هَلْ","يَا"],"feedback":"Lam governs the jussive present.","mode":"roles"},{"id":47,"book":"N2","pages":"5-7","section":"1–2","ar":"الْكَلِمَةُ وَأَقْسَامُهَا","title":"Word types revisited","rule":"Identify an ism by its signs (such as tanwīn or a preceding preposition), a verb by its time and signs, and a particle by its relation to other words.","urdu":"کلمہ اور اس کی قسمیں، اسم و فعل کی علامات۔","examples":[{"ar":"مِنْ","label":"Particle","note":"مِنْ is a preposition and governs a following noun."},{"ar":"الْمَسْجِدِ","label":"Noun","note":"الْمَسْجِدِ names a place and shows jarr in the example."},{"ar":"ذَهَبَ","label":"Verb","note":"ذَهَبَ expresses the past action of going."}],"question":"Which is an ism?","correct":"الْمَسْجِدِ","options":["الْمَسْجِدِ","مِنْ","ذَهَبَ"],"feedback":"The word accepts ال and jarr.","mode":"roles"},{"id":48,"book":"N2","pages":"7-11","section":"3–4","ar":"أَقْسَامُ الْجُمْلَةِ","title":"Sentence types and intentions","rule":"The book distinguishes complete/incomplete structures, nominal/verbal sentences and declarative/non-declarative utterances. Commands and questions are in the latter group.","urdu":"جملہ خبریہ و انشائیہ اور ان کی قسمیں۔","examples":[{"ar":"زَيْدٌ قَائِمٌ","label":"Statement","note":"The statement reports that Zayd is standing."},{"ar":"اُكْتُبْ","label":"Command","note":"The imperative asks a listener to write."},{"ar":"هَلْ ذَهَبْتَ؟","label":"Question","note":"The question requests information rather than making a statement."},{"ar":"يَا زَيْدُ","label":"Call","note":"The vocative calls the named person."}],"question":"Which is a question?","correct":"هَلْ ذَهَبْتَ؟","options":["هَلْ ذَهَبْتَ؟","زَيْدٌ قَائِمٌ","اُكْتُبْ"],"feedback":"هَلْ introduces a yes/no question.","mode":"timeline"},{"id":49,"book":"N2","pages":"11-14","section":"5","ar":"الْمُرَكَّبُ غَيْرُ الْمُفِيدِ","title":"Incomplete phrases","rule":"Distinguish iḍāfah, description and other types of incomplete compounds in the book. Iḍāfah makes the second noun genitive; description requires agreement.","urdu":"مرکب اضافی، توصیفی اور دوسری ترکیبیں۔","examples":[{"ar":"قَلَمُ زَيْدٍ","label":"Iḍāfah","note":"The pen belongs to Zayd, so the second noun is majrūr."},{"ar":"قَلَمٌ جَدِيدٌ","label":"Description","note":"The adjective جَدِيدٌ describes قَلَمٌ and agrees with it."},{"ar":"خَمْسَةَ عَشَرَ","label":"Number compound","note":"The compound numeral fifteen illustrates a different incomplete compound."}],"question":"Which is iḍāfah?","correct":"قَلَمُ زَيْدٍ","options":["قَلَمُ زَيْدٍ","قَلَمٌ جَدِيدٌ","زَيْدٌ قَائِمٌ"],"feedback":"Zayd is the muḍāf ilayh.","mode":"roles"},{"id":50,"book":"N2","pages":"14-15","section":"Numbers","ar":"الْأَعْدَادُ","title":"Numbers","rule":"Study cardinal and ordinal numbers separately. For three through ten, the counted noun is plural and genitive; the numeral typically uses the opposite gender.","urdu":"اعداد اصلی و ترتیبی؛ عدد و معدود۔","examples":[{"ar":"ثَلَاثَةُ كُتُبٍ","label":"Three books","note":"كُتُبٍ is the plural genitive counted noun; the numeral has the opposite gender to singular كِتَابٌ."},{"ar":"ثَلَاثُ سَيَّارَاتٍ","label":"Three cars","note":"سَيَّارَةٌ is feminine, so the numeral three appears without tāʾ marbūṭah."},{"ar":"الثَّالِثُ","label":"Third","note":"الثَّالِثُ is an ordinal: it indicates position, not quantity."}],"question":"Three books?","correct":"ثَلَاثَةُ كُتُبٍ","options":["ثَلَاثَةُ كُتُبٍ","ثَلَاثُ كُتُبٍ","ثَلَاثَةُ كِتَابٌ"],"feedback":"كِتَابٌ is masculine, so the numeral uses ة for three.","mode":"roles"},{"id":51,"book":"N2","pages":"15-19","section":"Nouns","ar":"الِاسْمُ وَأَقْسَامُهُ","title":"Noun classes and derivation","rule":"Review singular, dual and plural; jāmid, maṣdar and mushtaq; gender and definiteness. Distinguish semantic categories from case behavior.","urdu":"واحد، تثنیہ، جمع؛ جامد، مصدر، مشتق؛ معرفہ و نکرہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"رَجُلٌ","label":"Singular","note":"رَجُلٌ refers to one man."},{"ar":"رَجُلَانِ","label":"Dual","note":"رَجُلَانِ denotes two and shows the dual ending."},{"ar":"كِتَابَةٌ","label":"Maṣdar","note":"كِتَابَةٌ is a verbal noun, naming the act of writing."},{"ar":"كَاتِبٌ","label":"Mushtaq","note":"كَاتِبٌ is a derived active participle, a writer."}],"question":"Which is derived active participle?","correct":"كَاتِبٌ","options":["كَاتِبٌ","رَجُلٌ","رَجُلَانِ"],"feedback":"It follows فَاعِلٌ from كتب.","mode":"roles"},{"id":52,"book":"N2","pages":"19-24","section":"Inflection","ar":"الْإِعْرَابُ","title":"Case system and signs","rule":"Inflection may be displayed by a vowel, a letter or an estimated ending, depending on noun type. Compare singular, dual and sound plural endings.","urdu":"معرب، مبنی اور اسماء کے اعراب کی علامتیں۔","examples":[{"ar":"طَالِبٌ","label":"Rafʿ","note":"The singular rafʿ ending is ḍammah here."},{"ar":"طَالِبًا","label":"Naṣb","note":"The singular naṣb ending is fatḥah here."},{"ar":"طَالِبٍ","label":"Jarr","note":"The singular jarr ending is kasrah here."},{"ar":"طَالِبَانِ","label":"Dual rafʿ","note":"The dual uses alif and nūn in rafʿ."},{"ar":"طَالِبَيْنِ","label":"Dual naṣb/jarr","note":"The dual uses yāʾ and nūn in both naṣb and jarr."},{"ar":"مُسْلِمُونَ / مُسْلِمِينَ","label":"Case of sound masculine plural","note":"The raised form uses wāw; the accusative and genitive forms use yāʾ."},{"ar":"مُسْلِمَاتٌ / مُسْلِمَاتٍ","label":"Case of sound feminine plural","note":"The raised form uses ḍammah; accusative and genitive use kasrah."},{"ar":"أَبُوكَ / أَبَاكَ / أَبِيكَ","label":"Five nouns","note":"Under the required conditions, the five nouns use wāw for nominative, alif for accusative and yāʾ for genitive."}],"question":"Dual genitive ending?","correct":"طَالِبَيْنِ","options":["طَالِبَيْنِ","طَالِبَانِ","طَالِبُونَ"],"feedback":"The dual uses yāʾ in naṣb and jarr.","mode":"grammar"},{"id":53,"book":"N2","pages":"25-29","section":"Estimated cases","ar":"الْإِعْرَابُ التَّقْدِيرِيُّ","title":"Estimated inflection & special nouns","rule":"Some nouns have endings whose case vowel cannot be visibly expressed. Contrast the maqṣūr and manqūṣ and the book’s rules for inflection.","urdu":"اعراب تقدیری، اسم مقصور اور اسم منقوص۔","examples":[{"ar":"الْفَتَى","label":"Maqṣūr","note":"A maqṣūr noun ends in a necessary alif; the case vowels are estimated."},{"ar":"الْقَاضِي","label":"Manqūṣ","note":"A manqūṣ noun ends in a necessary yāʾ preceded by kasrah; its visible/estimated endings differ."},{"ar":"جَاءَ الْفَتَى","label":"Estimated rafʿ","note":"The subject الْفَتَى is raised even though the final ḍammah cannot be pronounced on the alif."},{"ar":"رَأَيْتُ الْقَاضِيَ","label":"Visible naṣb","note":"The accusative of this manqūṣ noun can show fatḥah visibly."},{"ar":"جَاءَ الْقَاضِي / مَرَرْتُ بِالْقَاضِي","label":"Estimated endings on the yāʾ noun","note":"Ḍammah and kasrah are estimated on the final yāʾ; fatḥah is visible in the accusative."}],"question":"Which is maqṣūr?","correct":"الْفَتَى","options":["الْفَتَى","الْقَاضِي","الْكِتَابُ"],"feedback":"It ends in a required alif.","mode":"roles"},{"id":54,"book":"N2","pages":"29-33","section":"Pronouns","ar":"الضَّمَائِرُ وَالْإِشَارَةُ وَالْمَوْصُولُ","title":"Fixed noun families","rule":"Pronouns, demonstratives and relative nouns belong to the noun category; many are fixed in form, with special dual forms and context-dependent grammatical positions.","urdu":"ضمائر، اسماء اشارہ اور اسماء موصولہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"أَنَا","label":"Pronoun","note":"أَنَا is a first-person detached pronoun; its form is fixed."},{"ar":"هَذِهِ","label":"Demonstrative","note":"هَذِهِ points to a near feminine singular noun."},{"ar":"الَّتِي","label":"Relative","note":"الَّتِي introduces a feminine singular relative clause."},{"ar":"كِتَابُهَا","label":"Attached pronoun","note":"The suffix ـهَا is a fixed attached pronoun in a genitive position here."}],"question":"Which is feminine singular relative?","correct":"الَّتِي","options":["الَّتِي","الَّذِي","هَذِهِ"],"feedback":"الَّتِي refers to a feminine singular antecedent.","mode":"roles"},{"id":55,"book":"N2","pages":"33-36","section":"Fixed forms","ar":"أَسْمَاءُ الْأَفْعَالِ وَالْأَصْوَاتِ","title":"Verb-nouns and sounds","rule":"Study fixed expressions that carry verbal meanings, sound expressions, adverbial nouns and allusive nouns as distinct types in the book.","urdu":"اسم فعل، اسم صوت، اسم ظرف، اسم کنایہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"صَهْ","label":"Be quiet (ism fiʿl)","note":"صَهْ is an ism fiʿl meaning be quiet."},{"ar":"هَيْهَاتَ","label":"Far away","note":"هَيْهَاتَ carries the verbal meaning of being far away."},{"ar":"كَمْ","label":"How many","note":"كَمْ is an allusive/interrogative expression; its syntax depends on usage."}],"question":"Which expresses a command to be quiet?","correct":"صَهْ","options":["صَهْ","كَمْ","هَيْهَاتَ"],"feedback":"صَهْ is an ism fiʿl amr.","mode":"roles"},{"id":56,"book":"N2","pages":"36-44","section":"Diptotes","ar":"غَيْرُ الْمُنْصَرِفِ","title":"Causes of diptote behavior","rule":"The text groups the causes of non-declension, including proper-name patterns, feminine marking, foreign origin, and certain plural patterns. Apply conditions before assigning case.","urdu":"غیر منصرف کے اسباب اور قواعد۔","examples":[{"ar":"أَحْمَدُ","label":"Proper name on verb pattern","note":"أَحْمَدُ combines a proper name with a verb-like pattern."},{"ar":"إِبْرَاهِيمُ","label":"Foreign proper name","note":"إِبْرَاهِيمُ is a foreign proper name meeting the relevant diptote conditions."},{"ar":"مَسَاجِدُ","label":"Plural pattern","note":"مَسَاجِدُ follows a final plural pattern that prevents ordinary full declension."},{"ar":"فِي مَسَاجِدَ","label":"Jarr with fatḥah","note":"With no الـ or iḍāfah, جarr is shown by fatḥah after فِي."},{"ar":"بِالْمَسَاجِدِ / بِمَسَاجِدِ الْمَدِينَةِ","label":"Genitive kasrah restored","note":"A diptote takes kasrah in genitive when it has the definite article or is a muḍāf."}],"question":"Which is jarr for indefinite مَسَاجِدُ?","correct":"فِي مَسَاجِدَ","options":["فِي مَسَاجِدَ","فِي مَسَاجِدٍ","فِي مَسَاجِدُ"],"feedback":"This plural pattern is diptote without ال or iḍāfah.","mode":"roles"},{"id":57,"book":"N2","pages":"45-47","section":"Subjects","ar":"الْفَاعِلُ وَنَائِبُ الْفَاعِلِ","title":"Subject and deputy subject","rule":"A fāʿil is raised by its active verb; the nāʾib al-fāʿil takes the raised role in a passive construction. Distinguish semantic agent from grammatical role.","urdu":"فاعل اور نائب فاعل کے قواعد۔","examples":[{"ar":"فَتَحَ زَيْدٌ الْبَابَ","label":"Active","note":"The verb is active. Zayd is raised and the door is accusative as object."},{"ar":"فُتِحَ الْبَابُ","label":"Passive","note":"The passive verb does not name the agent; the door moves into the raised role."},{"ar":"الْبَابُ","label":"Deputy subject","note":"الْبَابُ is now the deputy subject and carries ḍammah."}],"question":"Which is deputy subject in the passive example?","correct":"الْبَابُ","options":["الْبَابُ","زَيْدٌ","فُتِحَ"],"feedback":"The original object becomes the raised deputy subject.","mode":"grammar"},{"id":58,"book":"N2","pages":"47-50","section":"Nominal sentence","ar":"الْمُبْتَدَأُ وَالْخَبَرُ","title":"Nominal sentence analysis","rule":"A basic nominal sentence has a mubtadaʾ and khabar. The predicate may be a single word, a sentence or a phrase functioning as the predicate.","urdu":"مبتدا و خبر اور خبر کی صورتیں۔","examples":[{"ar":"الْبَيْتُ","label":"Mubtadaʾ","note":"الْبَيْتُ is the entity about which a statement is made."},{"ar":"كَبِيرٌ","label":"Khabar","note":"كَبِيرٌ is the information supplied about the house."},{"ar":"الْبَيْتُ كَبِيرٌ","label":"Complete sentence","note":"Together these two words make a complete nominal sentence."}],"question":"Which is the predicate?","correct":"كَبِيرٌ","options":["كَبِيرٌ","الْبَيْتُ","الْ"],"feedback":"It tells something about the subject.","mode":"roles"},{"id":59,"book":"N2","pages":"50-54","section":"Governors","ar":"النَّوَاسِخُ","title":"Inna, kāna, mā/lā and near-action verbs","rule":"Different governing expressions affect the nominal sentence differently. Study ḥurūf mushabbahah bil-fiʿl, afʿāl nāqiṣah, the book’s mā/lā constructions and afʿāl muqārabah in their own sections.","urdu":"حروف مشبہ بالفعل، افعال ناقصہ، ما و لا اور افعال مقاربہ۔","examples":[{"ar":"الطَّالِبُ حَاضِرٌ","label":"Base","note":"Both the noun and predicate begin in the raised case."},{"ar":"إِنَّ الطَّالِبَ حَاضِرٌ","label":"Inna","note":"After إِنَّ, الطَّالِبَ is accusative and حَاضِرٌ remains raised."},{"ar":"كَانَ الطَّالِبُ حَاضِرًا","label":"Kāna","note":"After كَانَ, الطَّالِبُ remains raised and حَاضِرًا is accusative."},{"ar":"كَادَ الطَّالِبُ يَسْقُطُ","label":"Near action","note":"كَادَ expresses near occurrence; its predicate here is a present-verb clause."},{"ar":"مَا زَيْدٌ قَائِمًا","label":"Mā resembling laysa","note":"In the Hijazi usage and under its conditions, mā raises its noun and gives accusative to its predicate."},{"ar":"لَا رَجُلَ فِي الْبَيْتِ","label":"Categorical negative lā","note":"Lā that negates an entire class governs an indefinite noun, here built on fatḥah in the position of accusative."}],"question":"Which shows اسم إنّ?","correct":"الطَّالِبَ","options":["الطَّالِبَ","الطَّالِبُ","حَاضِرٌ"],"feedback":"Inna governs its ism in naṣb.","mode":"grammar"},{"id":60,"book":"N2","pages":"54-61","section":"Accusatives","ar":"الْمَفَاعِيلُ وَالْمُنَادَى","title":"Objects and calling","rule":"Identify the direct object, absolute object, purpose, accompaniment, adverb and vocative. Vocatives and specialized constructions have detailed case and form rules.","urdu":"مفاعیل، منادی اور متعلقہ منصوبات۔","examples":[{"ar":"قَرَأْتُ كِتَابًا","label":"Direct object","note":"كِتَابًا is the object that was read."},{"ar":"جَلَسْتُ جُلُوسًا","label":"Cognate object","note":"جُلُوسًا repeats the meaning of the verb as a cognate object."},{"ar":"يَا زَيْدُ","label":"Vocative","note":"يَا calls Zayd. The proper-name vocative is built on ḍammah in this construction."},{"ar":"سِرْتُ لَيْلًا","label":"Time","note":"لَيْلًا locates the action in time."},{"ar":"قُمْتُ إِكْرَامًا لِلضَّيْفِ","label":"Object of purpose","note":"إِكْرَامًا explains why I stood up; it is the accusative object of purpose."},{"ar":"سِرْتُ وَالنَّهْرَ","label":"Object of accompaniment","note":"النَّهْرَ follows the wāw of accompaniment: I walked alongside the river."},{"ar":"جَلَسْتُ أَمَامَ الْبَيْتِ","label":"Adverb of place","note":"أَمَامَ is accusative and specifies where I sat. الْبَيْتِ is its genitive dependent."}],"question":"Which is a cognate object?","correct":"جُلُوسًا","options":["جُلُوسًا","كِتَابًا","لَيْلًا"],"feedback":"It is the maṣdar of جَلَسَ.","mode":"roles"},{"id":61,"book":"N2","pages":"61-67","section":"Adverbials","ar":"الظَّرْفُ وَالْحَالُ وَالتَّمْيِيزُ","title":"Adverb, state and specification","rule":"A ẓarf locates an action in time/place; ḥāl gives a state; tamyīz removes an ambiguity. Use the book’s rules and exceptions for their form.","urdu":"ظرف، حال، تمییز اور ان کے قواعد۔","examples":[{"ar":"جَلَسْتُ أَمَامَ الْبَيْتِ","label":"Place","note":"أَمَامَ locates the sitting in space; الْبَيْتِ is its genitive complement."},{"ar":"جَاءَ زَيْدٌ ضَاحِكًا","label":"State","note":"ضَاحِكًا expresses Zayd's condition at the time he arrived."},{"ar":"ازْدَادَ زَيْدٌ عِلْمًا","label":"Specification","note":"عِلْمًا specifies in what respect Zayd increased."}],"question":"What is عِلْمًا in the third example?","correct":"تَمْيِيزٌ","options":["تَمْيِيزٌ","فَاعِلٌ","ظَرْفٌ"],"feedback":"It specifies in what respect Zayd increased.","mode":"roles"},{"id":62,"book":"N2","pages":"67-72","section":"Exception & genitive","ar":"الِاسْتِثْنَاءُ وَالْإِضَافَةُ","title":"Exception and genitive governance","rule":"Exception case depends on whether the construction is complete and affirmative or negative. Iḍāfah and prepositions govern jarr; iḍāfah has further types.","urdu":"مستثنیٰ، مجرورات، اضافت اور اس کی قسمیں۔","examples":[{"ar":"جَاءَ الْقَوْمُ إِلَّا زَيْدًا","label":"Complete affirmative exception","note":"The complete affirmative exception places the excluded name in naṣb."},{"ar":"كِتَابُ الطَّالِبِ","label":"Iḍāfah","note":"The second noun الطَّالِبِ is majrūr because of iḍāfah."},{"ar":"مِنَ الْبَيْتِ","label":"Preposition","note":"مِنَ governs الْبَيْتِ in jarr."},{"ar":"مَا جَاءَ إِلَّا زَيْدٌ","label":"Incomplete negative exception","note":"There is no stated excluded-from group; the word after إِلَّا takes the case required by its function. Here it is the subject."}],"question":"Which is the excluded noun in the first example?","correct":"زَيْدًا","options":["زَيْدًا","الْقَوْمُ","جَاءَ"],"feedback":"Zayd is excluded from the arriving group.","mode":"roles"},{"id":63,"book":"N2","pages":"72-76","section":"Dependents","ar":"التَّوَابِعُ","title":"Adjectives, emphasis, apposition, conjunction","rule":"The follower inherits case from its antecedent. A true adjective agrees with its noun in case, definiteness, gender and number, subject to the agreement rules.","urdu":"توابع: صفت، تاکید، بدل، عطف بیان اور عطف نسق۔","examples":[{"ar":"مَرَرْتُ بِرَجُلٍ صَالِحٍ","label":"Adjective","note":"Both رَجُلٍ and its adjective صَالِحٍ are genitive after بِـ."},{"ar":"جَاءَ زَيْدٌ نَفْسُهُ","label":"Emphasis","note":"نَفْسُهُ reinforces the identity of the raised subject."},{"ar":"جَاءَ زَيْدٌ وَعَمْرٌو","label":"Conjunction","note":"عَمْرٌو is joined to زَيْدٌ and follows its raised case."},{"ar":"جَاءَ أَخُوكَ زَيْدٌ","label":"Apposition (badal)","note":"زَيْدٌ clarifies the identity of أَخُوكَ and follows its nominative case."}],"question":"Which adjective is correct after بِرَجُلٍ?","correct":"صَالِحٍ","options":["صَالِحٍ","صَالِحٌ","صَالِحًا"],"feedback":"It follows the genitive noun.","mode":"grammar"},{"id":64,"book":"N2","pages":"76-82","section":"Derivatives","ar":"الْمَصْدَرُ وَالْمُشْتَقَّاتُ","title":"Governing verbal nouns and participles","rule":"A maṣdar, active participle, passive participle or adjectival derivative can perform grammatical work under the conditions explained in the book.","urdu":"مصدر، اسم فاعل، اسم مفعول، صفت مشبہہ اور اسم تفضیل کا عمل۔","examples":[{"ar":"ضَرْبٌ","label":"Maṣdar","note":"ضَرْبٌ names the act without a tense; under conditions it may govern complements."},{"ar":"ضَارِبٌ","label":"Active participle","note":"ضَارِبٌ names the doer and may work like an active verb under the book's conditions."},{"ar":"مَضْرُوبٌ","label":"Passive participle","note":"مَضْرُوبٌ identifies what received the action and may work like a passive verb under conditions."},{"ar":"أَكْبَرُ","label":"Comparative","note":"أَكْبَرُ expresses comparison; its construction has its own rules."}],"question":"Which is the passive participle?","correct":"مَضْرُوبٌ","options":["مَضْرُوبٌ","ضَارِبٌ","ضَرْبٌ"],"feedback":"It denotes the recipient of the action.","mode":"roles"},{"id":65,"book":"N2","pages":"83-85","section":"Verbs","ar":"الْأَفْعَالُ","title":"Mental, transformation, praise and blame verbs","rule":"The book differentiates afʿāl qulūb and taṣyīr from praise/blame and exclamation constructions. Some of the former govern two objects originating from subject and predicate.","urdu":"افعال قلوب و تصییر، مدح و ذم، تعجب۔","examples":[{"ar":"ظَنَنْتُ زَيْدًا قَائِمًا","label":"Mental verb","note":"ظَنَنْتُ takes two objects here, زَيْدًا and قَائِمًا, corresponding to a nominal assertion."},{"ar":"جَعَلْتُ الطِّينَ خَزَفًا","label":"Transformation","note":"جَعَلْتُ expresses transformation of clay into pottery and governs two objects here."},{"ar":"نِعْمَ الرَّجُلُ زَيْدٌ","label":"Praise","note":"نِعْمَ expresses praise; this is not the same two-object construction."},{"ar":"بِئْسَ الْخُلُقُ الْكَذِبُ","label":"Blame verb","note":"بِئْسَ introduces blame, الْخُلُقُ is its subject and الْكَذِبُ is the particular thing blamed."}],"question":"Which is the second object in ظَنَنْتُ زَيْدًا قَائِمًا?","correct":"قَائِمًا","options":["قَائِمًا","زَيْدًا","ظَنَنْتُ"],"feedback":"It originated as the predicate of the nominal statement.","mode":"roles"},{"id":66,"book":"N2","pages":"86-90","section":"Prepositions","ar":"حُرُوفُ الْجَرِّ","title":"Prepositions and their meanings","rule":"Prepositions govern the following noun in jarr. Learn the different meanings the text assigns to مِنْ، إِلَى، فِي، عَلَى، باء، لام and others.","urdu":"حروف جارّہ اور ان کے مختلف معانی۔","examples":[{"ar":"مِنَ الْبَيْتِ","label":"From","note":"مِنْ marks a starting point: from the house."},{"ar":"إِلَى الْمَسْجِدِ","label":"To","note":"إِلَى marks the destination: to the mosque."},{"ar":"فِي الْمَسْجِدِ","label":"In","note":"فِي marks an interior location: inside the mosque."},{"ar":"عَلَى الْمَكْتَبِ","label":"On","note":"عَلَى marks a position on or above something: on the desk."}],"question":"Which means to the mosque?","correct":"إِلَى الْمَسْجِدِ","options":["إِلَى الْمَسْجِدِ","مِنَ الْبَيْتِ","فِي الْمَسْجِدِ"],"feedback":"إلى indicates direction toward a limit.","mode":"position"},{"id":67,"book":"N2","pages":"91-93","section":"Verb moods","ar":"نَوَاصِبُ وَجَوَازِمُ الْمُضَارِعِ","title":"Mood governors","rule":"Distinguish the particles which put the present verb in naṣb or jazm. The ending may be a vowel, deletion of nūn, or deletion of a weak radical according to the verb.","urdu":"نواصب و جوازم مضارع۔","examples":[{"ar":"يَكْتُبُ","label":"Rafʿ","note":"The ordinary indicative singular verb ends in ḍammah."},{"ar":"لَنْ يَكْتُبَ","label":"Naṣb","note":"لَنْ governs naṣb, making the final vowel fatḥah."},{"ar":"لَمْ يَكْتُبْ","label":"Jazm","note":"لَمْ governs jazm, here with sukūn on the final radical."},{"ar":"لَمْ يَرْمِ","label":"Weak radical deletion","note":"A final weak radical is removed in jazm: يَرْمِي becomes لَمْ يَرْمِ."},{"ar":"لَنْ يَكْتُبُوا / لَمْ يَكْتُبُوا","label":"Five verbs in naṣb and jazm","note":"Both governors delete the indicative nūn in the five verbs; the particles differ in meaning."},{"ar":"لَمْ يَدْعُ / لَمْ يَخْشَ","label":"Weak final jussive","note":"In the jussive of a weak-final imperfect the final weak letter is deleted."}],"question":"Which shows naṣb?","correct":"لَنْ يَكْتُبَ","options":["لَنْ يَكْتُبَ","لَمْ يَكْتُبْ","يَكْتُبُ"],"feedback":"Lan is a nāṣib.","mode":"grammar"},{"id":68,"book":"N2","pages":"93-98","section":"Particles","ar":"حُرُوفُ الْعَطْفِ وَغَيْرُهَا","title":"Conjunction and other particles","rule":"Recognize conjunction, answer, explanation, specification, verbal noun and question particles. Compare meanings rather than assuming all particles govern case.","urdu":"حروف عطف، جواب، تفسیر، تخصیص، مصدریہ اور استفہام۔","examples":[{"ar":"وَ","label":"And","note":"وَ connects elements without necessarily specifying their sequence."},{"ar":"ثُمَّ","label":"Then","note":"ثُمَّ joins events with order and an interval."},{"ar":"نَعَمْ","label":"Yes","note":"نَعَمْ is an affirmative response rather than a case governor."},{"ar":"هَلْ","label":"Question","note":"هَلْ introduces a question; it does not itself assign a case ending."},{"ar":"أَوْ / فَـ","label":"More conjunctions","note":"أَوْ can express choice; فَ often indicates immediate sequence. Their uses depend on context."}],"question":"Which denotes sequence with delay?","correct":"ثُمَّ","options":["ثُمَّ","وَ","هَلْ"],"feedback":"Thumma marks succession with an interval.","mode":"timeline"},{"id":69,"book":"N2","pages":"98-101","section":"Final particles","ar":"حُرُوفُ النَّفْيِ وَالتَّنْبِيهِ","title":"Negation, calling, emphasis and endings","rule":"The last sections review particles of negation, calling, prompting, emphasis and tanwīn. Use the original lists to identify their meanings and grammatical effects.","urdu":"حروف نفی، ندا، تنبیہ، تاکید اور تنوین۔","examples":[{"ar":"مَا كَتَبَ","label":"Past negation","note":"مَا negates the past verb without changing its morphology."},{"ar":"يَا زَيْدُ","label":"Calling","note":"يَا introduces a vocative construction."},{"ar":"أَلَا","label":"Attention","note":"أَلَا can draw attention to what follows."},{"ar":"كِتَابٌ","label":"Tanwīn","note":"Tanwīn is the doubled written short-vowel sign, pronounced with a final n in connected speech."}],"question":"Which is a calling particle?","correct":"يَا","options":["يَا","مَا","أَلَا"],"feedback":"Yāʾ is used for calling.","mode":"timeline"}]};
